d or a chicken I  don't know but this this is a well not  so how to improve that of course you can  look for a better model or you can  really train your your own model to have  more control and our one of our ideas  here that is not yet done but it's the  idea for experiment it's a really used  style transfer as a method of for data  augmentation because I I had this idea  before when working on one of this  year's data Science Bowl problem with no  detecting nuclei it didn't work it  didn't help but here data augmentation  with site transfer actually you might be  working because what we can do we can  choose some object detection model  download weights download the the data  set perhaps choose a subset with  specific categories there and pass it  through a site transfer offline with a  style from for example the 19th century  paintings and then continue training on  that that subset to see if it helps with  very difficult difficult objects so  that's that's our idea and that's what's  happening now in terms that we are  preparing the code we are working on  some filtering methods we have a  refining the predictions we need to have  some improvement in front end but we are  planning to really have a test in Museum  so I hope in the near future I will be  able to invite you all to the museum to  test test the game and if you'd like to  read more please go to the blog post so  that's the first part of the  intersection of art and AI the second  part is more I think higher level and as  I told you before I'm not going to give  you like definite answers but I would  like to show you some observations so  probably all of you have been involved  in some creative processes but maybe not  analyze creativity as a process itself  but what we know is that creativity  involves exploration randomness  playfulness  and there is no stress there is no space  for stress so there should be a space  for free exploration without judgments  so it's a space where we can make  mistakes and nobody is judging us  contrary to their face of execution when  once we have an idea and we need to  execute it and we need to develop it  then really need really need discipline  there was a nice talk about creativity  by John Cleese on YouTube I don't know  if it's still there but if there is any  are interested I can recommend it now  people thought about creative process  like long time before and this this  representation was done by hermann von  helmholtz  and Henri Poincare so in creative  process I mean you can actually identify  very Universal stages and this comes  from people who are working arts science  and doesn't matter ok you have a stage  of saturation you have a stage of  incubation and illumination sometimes we  add to this a verification now  saturation is when you define the  problem you gather information  everything you can about the problem you  dig in into the problem ok  you can compare it to the creation of  some kind of data set of experiences  related to the task now later you have a  stage of incubation which is  subconscious and it's unpredictable in  terms of time it's not variety that  you're gonna find a solution of a  problem that you're gonna find and a  brilliant idea but when it when you find  it it usually comes as a ha moment a  moment of elimination and that's a  signal from subconscious to conscious  ok so it may find you when you are  walking home from grocery store and  finally you know I found the idea later  on you may verify it at home this  happens for example in scientific  discovery when you have an idea and you  need to check some additional  calculations and so on but this is a  very general stage now an example from  song writing maybe some writing is not  the easiest way of talking about  creativity because it's quite complex  process but I like writing songs so I'll  give you a simple  example now I imagine you have to write  a song about poor burns story related  some building and a story from a city  it's actually from my hometown so I was  working on that I was I had a nice idea  for the chorus and I was digging into  the reading stories related to that  building to that particular history and  then I left it aside because I didn't  have a idea for the first verse so I  left it aside and in the meantime in my  workplace actually I put on a display  one of the images related to the story  and it was there for weeks and at some  point I came with the idea of first  verse which actually if you can read it  is about the bright light so actually it  resembles the image that I was using to  enhance it in the meantime now of course  it's shining example doesn't mean much  you can look at process of songwriting  creating lyrics from AI perspective and  there are many projects that do that  quick Google search shows you this  project and what they did they used LSD  aims for generating lyrics and they say  that what you need in the model is to  see the word or phrase and the model  will generate a lyrics in a style of a  specific artist so yeah that's not not  something maybe unexpected but if you  write as songwriters how they do work on  songs and creation of lyrics it's  actually a deeper process because it's  not on the layer of lyrics while lyrics  is the end product yes there is a very  good book on writing lyrics and there is  a very good very popular exercise on  creative writing I can recommend it to  you  it's called object writing and it's a  very short exercise that it's creative  and the idea is that you take a seed  word you can take it from a dictionary  you can take it from a website object  writing calm  run by Pat Patterson the author of the  book and then what you have to do is to  generate a scenario inspired by the word  so I tell you for example coffee and  things that appear so you create a  stream of consciousness the stream of  associations there's a very crucial  assumption here that you have to stay  stands about so you create you generate  a world around that concept and you stay  close to your experiences related to the  senses that's also important from the  perspective of the lyrics because senses  and these experiment sexual experiences  are something that everyone can relate  to  that's why they are very powerful in the  lyrics now you do that for 10 minutes  only and this is not more not less and  the purpose of this exercise is to make  it easier to you to dig into and to dive  into your imaginations and easily  generate the world's and I would like to  see something like that some kind of  experiment like that  in the domain of machine learning  because the end product is the same as  the project from the previous slide but  you can see that the process itself is  different it's image based or sensual  based it's the the lyrics is the upper  layer here and maybe you heard about  this this is a project that was  announced of the products announced this  week Promethean AI and it's a product  from a game industry which is a tool for  artists that general generates templates  of world of course it's not as as good  as humans but the aim of this is to  generate like the the template on which  you can all work okay so in a sense it's  also we see that these things have  started to happen in in the domain of  MMA I okay but coming back to the  creative process and trying to  understand it  and I would like to dig into it because  I would like to understand better human  creative process and I think that  machine learning experiments can perhaps  help help us doing that so we had a  situation we have incubation and  illumination and what is interesting is  the stage going from incubation to  elimination because if you go from  subconscious to conscious then the  signal must be very strong  that's one observation and as a  physicist when I hear about that I think  maybe there are some resonant effect  that promotes really enhances some  specific signal the other observation is  that once you find the good the one idea  you have this gut feeling that this is  the one and you do not search more so  perhaps from the computer from the  physical process you've had some found  some minimum or you've been rewarded and  also that there is no guarantee that  you're gonna find the solution so it's  like finding a minimum maybe you will  not find a minimum so maybe there's some  kind of multi-dimensional space and  there is no guarantee that you find a  final solution and there are multiple  probably good solutions so there is no  guarantee that there is just one  solution to your task okay so maybe we  could think about this also in terms of  a game and in terms of for example  reinforcement learning where you have  agent playing in the environment sending  actions and interesting rewards maybe  the the brain or this is a process that  somehow is kind of like a game but if  it's a like a game then the question is  what is the environment and what is  being rewarded okay so the environment  could be brought for for instance the  space of concepts or our experiences or  our memory but if you have a space then  we would need we need also concept of  the proximity or the measure of the  distance now if you treat the memory  like a huge database of experiences than  perhaps proximity is related to  coherence of various concepts okay now  the other thing what is being rewarded  often we say that creative process of  creative ideas are the ones that bring  very  and things together that is something  that is unexpected okay but we often say  well we often see that these ideas that  the best ones are the simple ones there  is also a concept of familiarity and I  will give you maybe example in a moment  and what I think it's important is that  not every random combination of two  distant concepts is is interesting so  it's not only about exploring the space  and finding putting distant things  together so I'll just give you a tiny  example here so imagine that you have a  task of writing a song that is very  short and catchy but the subject is  transparency and public information so  this is the real-life experience I I did  this so I sometimes support one of the  NGOs in Poland and they work with these  topics so as you can imagine this is not  the most romantic topic for a song but  anyway we managed I managed to it was a  songwriting challenge for me but the  solution was that I wrote a short song  based on tongue twisters and now we may  ask what is the relation been turned  from twisters and you may think that  these two things are very decent  concepts so how you bring them together  and why this is a good solution and it  turns out that there is a actually  multiple paths how you can connect these  two ideas they're very dissonant yes but  you can bring a mutual context for them  to interact together and there's also a  sense of familiarity for the tongue  twisters because this is something that  everyone heard of okay so I think this  is my last slide showing you that from  one concept you can go to another one  which is very different multiple ways  and perhaps this is something that could  be one of the things that is being  rewarded in such processes that if you  have  you search for a random leaf for a very  distant concept and then you try to link  those concepts and if you think in terms  of machine learning models imagine  they'll have a model that it's object  detection and you have thousands of  categories and you can ask okay which  categories are closer to each other in  the space of categories in the space of  representations and on that you you may  search well on that basis you could  perhaps design a simple experiment that  could mimic this or that could represent  this these ideas thank you very much  [Applause]
 

